    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nWith ui2 enabled and `'shm'` omitting `\"F\"`, file read messages are emitted with a bogus `\"'replacement string' item\"` message.\r\n\r\n### Details\r\n\r\nThis occurs with ui2 active, because `readfile()` builds up a fileinfo message into `IObuff`, which is later overwritten while also being emitted:\r\n\r\n```\r\nreadfile()                                 fileio.c\r\n  msg_trunc(IObuff, ...)                   message.c\r\n    msg(ts, ...)                           message.c\r\n      msg_keep(ts, ...)                    message.c\r\n        msg_end()                          message.c\r\n          msg_ext_ui_flush()               message.c\r\n            ui_call_event(...)             ui.c\r\n              [ui2 Lua handler]            vim/_core/ui2/messages.lua\r\n                nvim_buf_set_lines(...)    api/buffer.c\r\n                  check_string_array(...)  api/private/validate.c\r\n                    snprintf(IObuff, ...)  <-- clobbers IObuff while `ts` still points into it\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn more detail, the clobber is done by the ui2 `msg_show` handler, called from `readfile()`. The handler calls `nvim_buf_set_lines()` which eventually calls `check_string_array()`, clobbering `IObuff`.\r\n\r\n(`check_string_array()` writes into `IObuff` (a global), even when all items in the array pass validation, so even when `IObuff` is never used for display).\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nUse a local `char[]` instead of IObuff.\r\n\r\nAI-assisted",
    "closed_at": "2026-11-02T11:17:22Z",
